SqlStudio 8.0
-------------------

SqlStudio is a complete database modeling, administration and development program for MySQL.


This tree contains the source files for the SqlStudio 8.0.

SqlStudio is an extensible visual database design tool.


Buiding on Linux
----------------

To build SqlStudio on Ubuntu and other Debian based distros:

- Remove iodbc, if necessary

  $> sudo apt-get remove iodbc

- Install dependencies
  
  $> sudo apt-get install build-essential cmake cmake-data autoconf automake pkg-config libtool libzip-dev libxml2-dev \
                          libsigc++-2.0-dev libglade2-dev libglu1-mesa-dev libgl1-mesa-glx \
                          mesa-common-dev libmysqlclient-dev libmysqlcppconn-dev uuid-dev libpixman-1-dev libpcre3-dev \
                          libpango1.0-dev libcairo2-dev python-dev \
                          libboost-dev mysql-client python-pysqlite2 libsqlite3-dev \
                          swig libvsqlitepp-dev libgdal-dev libgtk-3-dev libgtkmm-3.0-dev libssl-dev \
                          libsecret-1-dev libproj-dev

* X - put the proper value for your system

- Extract the sources into a directory

  $> tar xvf sql-studio-community-<version>-src.tar.gz

- Go to the project and create a build directory

  $> cd sql-studio-community-<version>-src
  $> mkdir wb-build
  $> cd wb-build
  
- Build and install the project, and optionaly set the install prefix (should default to /usr/local)

  $> cmake [-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr] ..
  $> make
  $> sudo make install

- If you don't have permissions, you may install it on the current directory

  $> make install DESTDIR=.
  

For complete and up-to-date instructions, visit https://www.dev4fun.net/documentation/

Note about swig:
- You will need swig 3.0 to build WB

Note about ANTLR:
- ANTLR 4.13.2 is required to generate the files for the MySQL Parser. Download antlr-4.13.2-complete.jar from http://www.antlr.org/download.html.
  Then you'll need to pass it as cmake argument:
   -DWITH_ANTLR_JAR=<path_to_your_antlr_complete_file>

Note about iodbc:
 - Instead of iodbc you can use unixodbc. To do that you need to install unixodbc and add -DUSE_UNIXODBC=True to cmake.

Note about gdal:
 - Ubuntu Packages require unixodbc, so if you're using it, there won't be any problem. If you want to use iodbc, you'll need to build it from source.
 
Building on Windows
-------------------

SqlStudio for Windows is built for x64, the same way as in CI (the windows
job in .github/workflows/build.yml). The libraries and the app are built with
the v143 toolset: v145 (MSVC 14.5x) miscompiles the C++/CLI wrappers, and the
app fails at startup with "Bad method token".

- Install Visual Studio 2022 or 2026 with the "Desktop development with C++"
  and ".NET desktop development" workloads, the MSVC v143 build tools, C++ ATL
  and C++/CLI support for v143, and the .NET Framework 4.8 targeting pack.
  Also install PowerShell 7, Python 3 and MSYS2 (in C:\msys64), then the tools
  the cairo/glib build uses:

  PS> python -m pip install meson==1.12.1 ninja==1.13.2
  PS> C:\msys64\usr\bin\pacman.exe -Sy --needed mingw-w64-x86_64-pkgconf mingw-w64-x86_64-libffi
  PS> $env:PATH += ";C:\msys64\mingw64\bin"

- Build the 3rd-party bundle (MySQL client, Connector/C++, OpenSSL, Python,
  GDAL, cairo, glib, ...) and pyodbc, from the pyodbc source archive
  (pyodbc-5.3.0.tar.gz):

  PS> build/windows/build_3rdparty.ps1 -BundleDir C:\sqlstudio-bundle -PlatformToolset v143
  PS> build/windows/build_pyodbc.ps1 -BundleDir C:\sqlstudio-bundle -Sdist pyodbc-5.3.0.tar.gz -PlatformToolset v143

- Build the app into bin\x64\Release and check that every DLL it imports
  can be found:

  PS> build/windows/compile.ps1 -Action build -Configuration Release -BundleDir C:\sqlstudio-bundle -PlatformToolset v143
  PS> build/windows/check_dll_deps.ps1 -OutputDir bin\x64\Release

- Package it as a portable .zip (with the Visual C++ runtime) and an .msi
  built from it. WiX 7 requires accepting its EULA (Open Source Maintenance
  Fee terms, https://docs.firegiant.com/wix/osmf/); see build/msi/README.md:

  PS> build/zip/make_zip.ps1 -BinDir bin\x64\Release -OutDir dist
  PS> Expand-Archive dist\sql-studio-1.0.1-winx64.zip msi-payload
  PS> dotnet build build/msi/SqlStudio.wixproj -c Release -p:Edition=community -p:ProductVersion=1.0.1 `
          -p:InstallerPlatform=x64 -p:Platform=x64 "-p:PayloadDirectory=$PWD\msi-payload\SqlStudio 1.0.1 (winx64)" `
          -p:AcceptEula=wix7

  The .msi is written to build\msi\bin\x64\community\sql_studio.msi.



Building on macOS
-----------------

SqlStudio for macOS is built for Apple Silicon (arm64) and macOS 26 or later,
the same way as in CI (the macos job in .github/workflows/build.yml).

- Install Xcode and Homebrew, then the build tools and libraries:

  $> brew install swig autoconf automake libtool pkgconf openjdk cmake \
                  glib cairo pixman libpng libzip openssl@3 proj
  $> export PATH="$(brew --prefix openjdk)/bin:$PATH"

- Build the 3rd-party bundle (MySQL client, Connector/C++, GDAL, ANTLR runtime,
  libssh, ...):

  $> build/mac/build_3rdparty.sh --bundle "$HOME/sqlstudio-bundle-mac"

- Download the python.org installer for Python 3.12 (python-3.12.10-macos11.pkg),
  antlr-4.13.2-complete.jar and the pyodbc source archive (pyodbc-5.3.0.tar.gz),
  then lay out 3rd-party-ce/, which the Xcode project reads:

  $> build/mac/prepare_3rdparty_ce.sh --bundle "$HOME/sqlstudio-bundle-mac" \
         --python-pkg python-3.12.10-macos11.pkg \
         --antlr-jar antlr-4.13.2-complete.jar --pyodbc-sdist pyodbc-5.3.0.tar.gz

- Build the app:

  $> xcodebuild -project SqlStudio.xcodeproj -scheme SqlStudio -configuration Release \
         -arch arm64 ONLY_ACTIVE_ARCH=YES MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=26.0 \
         CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO -derivedDataPath build/xcode build

- Copy the libraries the app loads into it and package it as a .dmg:

  $> build/mac/bundle_app.py build/xcode/Build/Products/Release/SqlStudio.app \
         --search "$HOME/sqlstudio-bundle-mac/lib" --search 3rd-party-ce/lib
  $> build/mac/make_dmg.sh build/xcode/Build/Products/Release/SqlStudio.app SqlStudio.dmg

